.\" Manual page for the udevmon configuration file. Keep it in step with
.\" src/udevmon/config.py: tests/test_manpages.py fails when a key is missing.
.TH UDEVMON.TOML 5 "2026-09-21" "udevmon" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
udevmon.toml \- configuration file of udevmon
.SH DESCRIPTION
The configuration of
.BR udevmon (1)
is a TOML file, by default
.IR ~/.config/udevmon/config.toml
(under
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME
if that is set; the
.B \-\-config
option chooses another file).
It says which devices you care about, and which commands to run when something
happens to them.
.PP
You can edit the file by hand or with the
.B devices
.RB , " tags"
and
.B actions
commands of
.BR udevmon (1),
which keep your comments and formatting and save the previous version as
.IR config.toml.bak .
After a hand edit run
.B udevmon check
to validate it and
.B udevmon reload
to make a running daemon read it.
The editing commands reload the daemon themselves.
.PP
udevmon refuses to load a file that is not owned by you or that is writable by
group or others, because the file decides what gets executed.
Fix it with
.BR "chmod go\-w" .
The daemon starts with an empty configuration when the file does not exist;
.B udevmon check
reports it as an error.
.PP
The file has four kinds of table:
.TP
.B [daemon]
Optional settings.
.TP
.B [devices.NAME]
Named devices.
.TP
.B [tags.NAME]
Tags that select things by a rule.
.TP
.B [[actions]]
The commands to run.
.PP
Unknown keys, at any level, are errors, so a mistyped key never goes unnoticed.
Names of devices, tags and actions use only letters, digits,
.BR _ ,
.B .
and
.BR \- .
A name with a dot must be quoted in a header:
.BR [devices."usb.1"] .
.SH "[daemon]"
Every key is optional.
.TP
.B debounce
Number of seconds, default
.BR 1.0 .
An event that repeats the last event of the same thing (the same plugged-in
device or filesystem) and is of the same kind, within this time, is dropped
and logged.
Two things matching one device are not repeats of each other, and any other
event of the thing in between ends the repeat.
Must not be negative;
.B 0
turns it off.
.TP
.B max_workers
Integer of at least 1, default
.BR 4 .
How many events are handled in parallel.
Actions for one event run one after another.
.TP
.B udev_filters
List of udev filters, default
.BR ["usb/usb_device"] .
Each entry is
.IB subsystem /devtype
or just a subsystem.
Other filters (for example
.BR "input" )
are experimental and reach the device through its raw
.BI udev_ name
properties.
Changing it needs a daemon restart, not a reload.
.TP
.B script_dir
A folder, or a list of folders (\fB~\fR is expanded), where your own scripts live.
Default: none.
A command given by bare name is then looked for in these folders, in order, and if
it is in none of them, only in the standard system paths
.RB ( /usr/local/sbin ,
.BR /usr/local/bin ,
.BR /usr/sbin ,
.BR /usr/bin ,
.BR /sbin ,
.BR /bin ).
The daemon's own
.B PATH
is not used, so a command is found the same way under systemd and in a terminal.
The first executable file wins.
The lookup happens at every event, so a script added later is found without a
reload.
A command with a
.B /
in it, or that starts with a placeholder, is used as written.
Without
.B script_dir
bare names use the
.BR PATH .
.SH "[devices.NAME]"
A device is a name for one thing: a USB device or a filesystem.
.TP
.B match
Required, a non\-empty table of property names and exact values.
Values are strings (numbers are accepted and taken as strings).
A thing is this device when all the pairs are equal to its properties
(capitals do not matter).
See
.BR PROPERTIES .
.TP
.B description
Optional text.
.TP
.B tags
Optional explicit tags: a name or a list of names.
.TP
.B exclude_tags
Optional: a name or a list of names of rule tags this device opts out of.
A rule that would select a thing matching this device leaves that tag out, so the
tag's actions do not run for it.
The device stays named and its own actions still run.
Only rule selection is affected: a tag the device carries in
.BR tags ,
or that another matching device carries, is kept, and the same tag in both
.B tags
and
.B exclude_tags
is an error.
.TP
.B vars
Optional table of variables,
.BR "vars = { dest = \\"/srv/x\\" }" ,
which actions use as
.BR {var_dest} .
Names are lowercase letters, digits and
.BR _ ,
not starting with a digit; values are strings (integers become text).
See
.BR VARIABLES .
.TP
.B status
Optional.
Left out, empty or
.B "active"
means the normal state.
.B "disabled"
stops the device's own actions (the ones that name it in
.BR device ),
while actions reached through its tags still run.
.B "ignored"
stops everything: no action runs for it, however it is reached.
If a thing matches two devices and either is ignored, nothing runs.
A disabled or ignored device still counts as named (see
.BR "NAMED AND UNNAMED THINGS" ).
.PP
A device can also be written as a table with a sub\-table for the match
.RB ( [devices.NAME.match] ).
.SS "Match strength"
.B udevmon check
and the commands that create devices rate every match:
.TP
.B strong
It includes a serial number or a UUID.
It identifies exactly this device.
.TP
.B medium
Vendor ID, product ID and physical port
.RB ( port_path ).
It matches any device of that model in that port.
It says where the device is plugged in, not which device it is, so
.B scan
never suggests it (see
.BR "Devices without a serial number" ).
.TP
.B weak
Anything else, such as a filesystem label or only the model.
It matches every device with those values, and a warning is printed.
.PP
Serials that are obviously fake
.RB ( 0000000 ,
.BR 1234567890 ,
and similar) count as weak.
A device match that uses a property which changes from plug to plug
.RB ( mountpoint ,
.BR devnode ,
.BR syspath )
also draws a warning.
.SS "Devices without a serial number"
Some devices, YubiKeys for one, report no serial number, so nothing tells one unit from
another.
For them
.B udevmon scan
pre-selects nothing; it offers
.IP 1) 4
this model, in this port only
.RB ( vendor_id ,
.BR product_id ,
.BR port_path ):
another unit of the model in that port matches too, and the device stops matching in
another port or behind a hub;
.IP 2) 4
this model, in any port
.RB ( vendor_id ,
.BR product_id ):
it matches every unit of the model.
.PP
or a list of properties you type.
Neither is strong, so
.B scan
asks before saving.
.B udevmon present
marks such a device
.BR "[medium: no serial, matches the port]" ,
and
.B udevmon check
notes any device or tag rule that uses
.BR port_path .
.SH "[tags.NAME]"
A tag is a label that lets one action cover several devices.
There are two ways to give things a tag.
.PP
By
.BR tags
on a device (the explicit way): the device lists it, and no declaration is
needed.
.PP
By a rule, declared here.
A rule selects things by their properties, so you do not have to name each
one.
.TP
.B match
A table of property names and values.
A value can be a list, meaning \(lqany of\(rq.
Every key must match, and capitals do not matter.
In a rule (not in a device's
.BR match )
a value may use wildcards:
.B *
matches any run of characters (including none) and
.B ?
exactly one, and the whole value must match.
Everything else is literal, so a value that really contains a
.B *
or
.B ?
cannot be matched literally.
A value with a wildcard, like a list, never makes a rule strong.
Rules may use any property except those that change from plug to plug:
.BR mountpoint ,
.BR devnode ,
.BR syspath ,
.BR event ,
.BR device ,
.BR tag ,
.BR surprise ,
and the raw udev properties
.BR udev_devname ,
.BR udev_devpath ,
.BR udev_busnum ,
.BR udev_devnum ,
.B udev_seqnum
and
.BR udev_usec_initialized .
.TP
.B description
Optional text.
.PP
A tag without a
.B match
is valid, and contains only the devices that carry it explicitly.
An action on several tags runs if the thing has any of them, unless it sets
.BR "tag_mode = \\"all\\"" .
.PP
.nf
[tags.storage]
match = { fs_type = ["ext4", "exfat"] }
.fi
.SH "[[actions]]"
Each
.B [[actions]]
table is one action.
.TP
.B device
The named device (or a list of names) the action applies to.
Every name must exist.
.TP
.B tag
The tag (or a list of tags) the action applies to; it runs if the thing has any
of them.
.PP
An action has exactly one of
.B device
and
.BR tag .
.TP
.B on
Required: one event, or a list of events, from
.BR connected ,
.BR disconnected ,
.BR mounted ,
.BR unmounted ,
.BR removed ,
.B startup
and
.BR shutdown .
See
.BR EVENTS .
.TP
.B run
Required: the command, as a list of arguments (or a string that is split
shell\-style).
See
.BR "HOW COMMANDS ARE RUN" .
.TP
.B name
Optional, unique.
Without it the action is named after its first target and its position in the
file (for example
.BR backup\-disk\-2 ).
The commands that change or remove an action need a name; give it one by hand.
.TP
.B timeout
Seconds, greater than 0, default
.BR 60 .
An action that runs longer is terminated, together with any child processes it
started.
The timeout only applies while the command itself is running.
It cannot be combined with
.BR detach .
.TP
.B detach
Boolean, default false.
.B "detach = true"
starts the command and does not wait for it: there is no timeout, the actions after it
and a
.B shutdown
are not held up, its output is logged as usual and its exit status is logged when it
ends.
Use it when the command is itself a long-running program, for example
.BR "run = [\\"keepassxc\\", \\"{mountpoint}/pass.kdbx\\"]" .
See
.BR "PROGRAMS THAT KEEP RUNNING" .
.TP
.B lock
Optional name.
Actions that share a lock name never run at the same time, and run in the order
their events arrived.
See
.BR "RUNNING ACTIONS ONE AT A TIME" .
.TP
.B retries
Whole number, default 0.
When the command exits with a non-zero status it is run again, up to this many more
times.
See
.BR "TRYING AGAIN" .
.TP
.B retry_delay
Seconds, more than 0, default 1: how long to wait between attempts.
.TP
.B allow_unnamed
Boolean, only for actions that use
.BR tag .
Left out, it behaves as false.
See
.BR "NAMED AND UNNAMED THINGS" .
.TP
.B tag_mode
.B "any"
(the default) or
.BR "all" ,
only for actions that use
.BR tag .
With
.B "all"
the action runs only for things that have every tag it lists, whether explicit or
selected by a rule.
.B {tag}
is then the first tag in the list.
.TP
.B enabled
Boolean, default true.
.B "enabled = false"
keeps the action in the file but it never runs.
.SH EVENTS
.TP
.B connected
A USB device was plugged in.
.TP
.B disconnected
A USB device was unplugged.
.TP
.B mounted
A filesystem was mounted.
.TP
.B unmounted
A filesystem was unmounted, or its device disappeared while it was mounted.
.TP
.B removed
A mounted filesystem vanished without being unmounted first: the stick was
pulled out, or the drive failed.
It comes right after
.BR unmounted ,
which fires too.
.TP
.B startup
The device was already present when udevmon started.
It fires for every matching device each time the daemon starts, including after
a restart, but not after a reload.
.TP
.B shutdown
The device is still present when udevmon stops normally.
The daemon waits for these actions, up to each one's
.BR timeout .
They do not run after
.B SIGKILL
or a crash.
.PP
Pulling a mounted stick therefore produces
.BR unmounted ,
then
.BR removed ,
for its filesystem, and
.B disconnected
for the USB device, in any order relative to each other.
A clean eject produces only
.BR unmounted .
The
.B surprise
property tells them apart.
.PP
While automation is paused
.RB ( "udevmon pause" )
nothing runs, including
.B startup
and
.BR shutdown .
.SH PROPERTIES
Properties are used in
.BR match ,
in
.B {placeholders}
of a command and in
.B UDEVMON_
environment variables.
.PP
USB devices (events
.BR connected ,
.B disconnected
and, for things that are USB devices,
.B startup
and
.BR shutdown ):
.TP
.BR vendor_id ", " product_id
Lowercase hexadecimal, for example
.B 0781
and
.BR 5567 .
.TP
.B serial
For example
.BR 4C530001230815112433 .
The best identifier, when the device has one.
.TP
.BR vendor ", " model
Human\-readable names.
.TP
.B port_path
The physical port position, for example
.BR 3\-1.2 .
.TP
.BR devnode ", " syspath
For example
.IR /dev/bus/usb/003/007 .
.TP
.BR subsystem ", " devtype
The udev subsystem and device type.
.TP
.BI udev_ name
Every raw udev property, lowercased (for example
.BR udev_id_input_keyboard ).
In a command,
.BI { udev_NAME }
is filled in from the event; a thing that lacks the property makes the action be
skipped, with an error in the log, and
.B check
cannot catch a typo there.
.PP
Filesystems (events
.BR mounted ,
.BR unmounted ,
.BR removed ,
and
.B startup
and
.B shutdown
for filesystems):
.TP
.B fs_uuid
For example
.BR 3F2A\-91BC .
The best identifier.
.TP
.B part_uuid
The partition UUID (GPT disks).
.TP
.BR fs_label ", " fs_type
For example
.B BACKUP
and
.BR ext4 .
Labels are not unique.
.TP
.B devnode
For example
.IR /dev/sdb1 .
.TP
.B mountpoint
For example
.IR /media/me/BACKUP .
Present on
.BR mounted ,
.BR unmounted ,
.B removed
and mounted
.B startup
and
.B shutdown
events.
On
.B removed
the directory is gone; the value is only text.
.TP
.B surprise
.B 1
if the filesystem vanished while it was mounted,
.B 0
after a clean unmount.
Present on
.B unmounted
and
.BR removed .
It can be used in commands but not in tag rules.
.TP
.BR drive_vendor ", " drive_model ", " drive_serial
Those of the physical drive that holds the filesystem.
.PP
A property that is missing from a thing (a stick without a serial number, a
filesystem without a label) simply does not match.
.B udevmon present
lists everything present with these values.
.PP
On both kinds, added by udevmon itself:
.TP
.B instance
One value for each plug of a USB device or each mount of a filesystem, identical in
every event of it and new for the next plug or mount (for example
.B usb\-9878235508
or
.BR fs\-1758400000\-3 ).
It changes on every plug, so it cannot be used in tag rules.
See
.BR "MATCHING AN UNPLUG WITH ITS PLUG" .
.SH "MATCHING AN UNPLUG WITH ITS PLUG"
An action for
.B disconnected
often needs something the action for
.B connected
found out, for example which YubiKey it was (the key reports no serial number, so a
script asks
.BR ykman ).
udevmon gives every plug, and every mount, one value that is the same in all its events,
and a private folder to keep notes in:
.TP
.BI { instance } ", " UDEVMON_INSTANCE
The instance (see
.BR PROPERTIES ):
the same in
.BR connected ,
.BR startup ,
.B disconnected
and
.B shutdown
of a USB device, and in
.BR mounted ,
.BR startup ,
.BR unmounted ,
.B removed
and
.B shutdown
of a filesystem.
It is built on udev's
.B USEC_INITIALIZED
(different for every plug), or on a counter when the device has none; a new mount of a
filesystem is a new instance.
It is unique only while the daemon runs.
.TP
.BI { state_dir } ", " UDEVMON_STATE_DIR
A private folder,
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/udevmon/state/ INSTANCE ,
mode 0700, created before the first action of that instance runs.
A connect script writes into it and the disconnect script reads it.
It is removed after the actions of the instance's last event have finished:
.B disconnected
for a USB device;
.B unmounted
for a filesystem, or
.B removed
when it was pulled (the
.B unmounted
of a pull, with
.BR surprise=1 ,
is followed by
.BR removed ).
The whole
.I state
folder is wiped when the daemon starts and when it stops, so nothing outlives a restart;
.B startup
is where a script rebuilds what it needs.
.PP
Two plugged\-in things have two folders, so several keys work at once.
A detached action must not rely on the folder after the last event's actions have
finished, and an action of the connect event that is still running when the
disconnect's actions end may find it gone: give both the same
.B lock
to keep them apart.
There is no link between a USB device and its filesystems; each has its own instance.
.SH VARIABLES
A device's
.B vars
are values of your own that its actions can use:
.PP
.nf
[devices.photos]
match = { fs_uuid = "3F2A-91BC" }
tags = ["backup"]
vars = { dest = "/srv/backups/photos" }

[devices.music]
match = { fs_uuid = "7B10-44E0" }
tags = ["backup"]
vars = { dest = "/srv/backups/music" }

[[actions]]
tag = "backup"
on = ["mounted"]
run = ["~/bin/backup.sh", "{mountpoint}", "{var_dest}"]
.fi
.PP
.BI { var_NAME }
in a command is replaced by the variable
.I NAME
of the device the action runs for, and the environment gets
.BI UDEVMON_VAR_ NAME
(in capitals).
Which device that is: the listed device that applies, for an action with
.BR device ;
the first matching named device, for an action with
.BR tag .
.IP \(bu 3
A thing that matches no device (only a tag rule) has no variables, so an action
that uses one is skipped for it, and
.B check
warns about an
.B allow_unnamed
action that uses one.
.IP \(bu 3
A device that lacks a variable an action uses gets the action skipped, with an
error, never run with an empty value.
.B check
warns about every device an action can reach that does not define it.
.IP \(bu 3
A variable that no device defines is an error when the file is loaded, which
catches typos.
.IP \(bu 3
The values are yours, not the hardware's, so they count as safe in the
.B allow_unnamed
checks.
.SH "NAMED AND UNNAMED THINGS"
A tag rule can select things you never named, for example any ext4 drive that
someone plugs in.
That is useful, and it is what \(lqBadUSB\(rq attacks rely on, because the
properties a rule looks at are chosen by whoever built the device.
udevmon therefore separates two cases:
.IP \(bu 3
A
.B named
thing matches one of your
.B [devices.*]
tables.
Every action on its tag runs, whether it got the tag explicitly or through a
rule.
.IP \(bu 3
An
.B unnamed
thing matches a tag rule only.
Only actions with
.B "allow_unnamed = true"
run for it.
.PP
Naming a device is you vouching for it, and an action has to opt in before it
may run for hardware you have not vetted.
.PP
.nf
[[actions]]
tag = "storage"
on = ["mounted"]
allow_unnamed = true     # also runs for drives I never named
run = ["notify\-send", "Drive mounted"]

[[actions]]
tag = "storage"
on = ["mounted"]
allow_unnamed = false    # named devices only (the default)
run = ["~/bin/backup.sh", "{mountpoint}"]
.fi
.PP
Points to know:
.IP \(bu 3
.B allow_unnamed
only widens who may trigger an action; a named device is never restricted by
it.
.IP \(bu 3
Left out, the action behaves as false, and
.B udevmon check
prints a note for actions on rule tags so that you choose on purpose.
.IP \(bu 3
Keep
.B allow_unnamed
actions harmless (notifications, logging): they run on hardware whose labels,
model names and serial numbers are chosen by its maker, so do not let them read or
run anything from it.
.B check
warns when such an action puts those values in its command, or uses
.BR {device} ,
which does not exist for unnamed things.
.IP \(bu 3
Trust belongs to each thing, not to the whole stick.
A stick is a USB device and a filesystem; if you named only the filesystem,
its USB device is still unnamed.
.IP \(bu 3
.B startup
and
.B shutdown
follow the same rules, so an allowed action on a broad rule fires once for
every matching thing that is present, including system filesystems.
.SH "HOW COMMANDS ARE RUN"
.IP \(bu 3
.B run
is never passed to a shell: there are no pipes, globbing or variable expansion in
the command itself.
If you need a shell, ask for one:
.IP
.nf
run = ["sh", "\-c", 'cd "$1" && ls | wc \-l >> ~/count.log',
       "sh", "{mountpoint}"]
.fi
.IP \(bu 3
A leading
.B ~
in an argument is replaced by your home directory.
.IP \(bu 3
.BI { var_NAME }
is a variable of the device (see
.BR VARIABLES ).
.IP \(bu 3
.BI { name }
in an argument is replaced by the value of a property (see
.BR PROPERTIES ),
and also by
.BR {event} ,
.B {device}
(the name of the named device; not available for unnamed things) and
.B {tag}
(tag actions only).
The value is always one argument, so spaces or shell characters in a mount
point cannot break out of the command.
.IP \(bu 3
Only
.B {lowercase_names}
are placeholders.
Other braces
.RB ( ${HOME} ,
.BR "awk '{print $1}'" )
are left alone.
Write
.B {{
or
.B }}
for a literal brace.
.IP \(bu 3
If a placeholder is not available for the event (for example
.B {mountpoint}
on
.BR connected ),
.B udevmon check
reports it.
If it is missing at run time (for example
.B {fs_label}
on an unlabelled filesystem), the action is skipped with an error rather than
run with an empty value.
.IP \(bu 3
The same information is in the environment:
.BR UDEVMON_EVENT ,
.B UDEVMON_DEVICE
(when the thing is named),
.B UDEVMON_TAG
(tag actions) and
.BI UDEVMON_VAR_ NAME
for every variable of the device,
.B UDEVMON_STATE_DIR
(see
.BR "MATCHING AN UNPLUG WITH ITS PLUG" ),
and
.BI UDEVMON_ PROPERTY
for every property, for example
.BR UDEVMON_MOUNTPOINT ,
.B UDEVMON_SERIAL
and
.BR UDEVMON_SURPRISE .
Your own environment is passed on too.
.IP \(bu 3
Graphical programs work under the service as well: for every action udevmon
looks up your desktop session's
.BR DISPLAY ,
.BR WAYLAND_DISPLAY ,
.B XAUTHORITY
and
.BR DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS ,
from systemd's environment for your user or, if the desktop never put them
there, from one of your own running programs.
At the very beginning of a graphical login, before the desktop has a display,
an action waits for it, up to 30 seconds.
The log says which display is used.
.IP \(bu 3
Commands run as you, with your home directory as the working directory and no
standard input.
Their output goes to the log (the journal, under systemd).
.IP \(bu 3
Several actions for one event run one after another, in file order.
A failing action does not stop the next.
.IP \(bu 3
Without
.B script_dir
the daemon looks for the program with its own
.BR PATH ,
which under systemd is often shorter than your shell's.
Use
.B script_dir
or full paths in
.BR run .
.SH "PROGRAMS THAT KEEP RUNNING"
udevmon waits for the
.I command
to exit, not for everything it started.
A script that does its work, starts a program in the background
.RB ( "keepassxc &" ,
then
.BR disown )
and exits is finished when it exits: the program keeps running, and whatever it
writes to its output is still logged.
The
.B timeout
only applies while the script itself is running.
.PP
When the command is itself the long-running program, set
.BR detach .
A detached command runs in its own session and is never waited for.
.PP
Two limits.
Under the systemd service, stopping the service stops everything the actions started,
detached or not, because systemd ends the whole group; from a terminal they keep
running.
To start something that must outlive the service, run it through
.BR "systemd\\-run \\-\\-user \\-\\-scope" .
And a detached
.I script
runs entirely unattended, so it is not the place for work that must finish before
something else starts.
.SH "TRYING AGAIN"
A command that can fail for a moment, such as unmounting right after a desktop has
mounted and is still looking at a drive, can be run again:
.PP
.nf
[[actions]]
device = "linuxsys"
on = ["mounted"]
retries = 10
retry_delay = 1
run = ["udisksctl", "unmount", "\-b", "{devnode}"]
.fi
.PP
When the command exits with a non\-zero status it is run again after
.BR retry_delay ,
up to
.B retries
more times, and each failed attempt is logged.
It is never retried after a
.BR timeout ,
when the program cannot start, or when the action was skipped, because repeating those
rarely helps.
Each attempt has its own
.BR timeout ,
so the worst case is
.RI ( retries " + 1) x " timeout
plus the delays.
Retrying stops at once when automation is paused or the daemon is stopping, and a
.B lock
is held across all the attempts.
.B retries
and
.B retry_delay
cannot be combined with
.BR detach .
.SH "RUNNING ACTIONS ONE AT A TIME"
The actions of one event run one after another, but different events run at the same
time (up to
.BR max_workers ).
So the actions for a plug and for the unplug that follows can overlap.
Give the actions that must not overlap the same
.BR lock .
.PP
Actions that share a lock name never run at the same time, and they run in the order
their events arrived: a quick unplug cannot overtake its plug, even if a worker is
free.
Actions without a lock, and locks with other names, are not affected, and within one
event the actions still run in file order.
The lock lives inside udevmon: there is no lock file, and it does not protect against
the same script being run by hand.
.PP
The lock is held while the command runs and is released as soon as the command has
exited (for a
.B detach
action, as soon as it has started).
A waiting action occupies one of the
.B max_workers
workers, so keep locked actions short; every command has a
.BR timeout ,
so a stuck one holds the others for at most that long.
The log says when an action had to wait.
.PP
To choose by what an action does:
.IP \(bu 3
a short script, which may leave a helper running with
.BR & :
.B lock
only, held until the script exits;
.IP \(bu 3
the long-running program itself:
.BR "detach = true" ,
held until it has started;
.IP \(bu 3
critical work followed by starting a long-running program: two actions on the same
event, the first with
.B lock
(the work) and the second with
.B detach
(the launch), in that order.
.SH VALIDATION
.B udevmon check
and the daemon load the file with the same rules.
The file cannot be loaded (an
.B error:
and exit status 1, or
.B "invalid config"
in the log) when:
.IP \(bu 3
a key is unknown, a name is not made of letters, digits,
.BR _ ,
.B .
and
.BR \- ,
or a name is used twice for actions;
.IP \(bu 3
a device has no
.B match
or a non\-string value, a
.B status
other than the three allowed, an
.B exclude_tags
that is empty or lists a tag also in its
.BR tags ,
or
.B vars
that is not a table, has a name that is not lowercase letters, digits and
.BR _ ,
or has a value that is not text;
.IP \(bu 3
an action names a device that does not exist, has both or neither of
.B device
and
.BR tag ,
uses
.B allow_unnamed
with
.BR device ,
lists a bad event, has an empty
.BR run ,
an invalid
.BR timeout ,
a
.B detach
that is not a boolean or that comes with a
.BR timeout ,
a
.B lock
that is not a name, a
.B retries
that is not a whole number of 0 or more, a
.B retry_delay
that is not a number above 0, either of them with
.BR detach ,
a
.B tag_mode
other than
.B any
or
.BR all ,
or a placeholder that is not available for one of its events, including a
.BI { var_NAME }
that no device defines;
.IP \(bu 3
a tag rule uses a key that changes from plug to plug.
.PP
Problems that do not stop the file from loading are warnings and notes:
weak matches, a wildcard in a device's
.B match
(where it is literal), a tag that no device carries and that has no rule (so its
action never runs), a program that cannot be found or run, a device that lacks a
variable an action uses, an
.B allow_unnamed
action that uses a variable, an
.B exclude_tags
entry with nothing to opt out of, disabled things, and choices about
.BR allow_unnamed .
When a reload finds an invalid file, the daemon logs the error and keeps its old
configuration.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
[daemon]                       # every setting is optional
debounce = 1.0
max_workers = 4
udev_filters = ["usb/usb_device"]

[devices.backup\-disk]
description = "Weekly backup drive"
match = { fs_uuid = "3F2A\-91BC" }
tags = ["backup", "storage"]

[devices.yubikey]
match = { vendor_id = "1050", product_id = "0407", serial = "A1B2C3D4E5" }

[devices.hub]
match = { vendor_id = "1d6b", product_id = "0002", port_path = "usb1" }
status = "ignored"             # nothing runs for it

[tags.storage]                 # a rule tag
description = "Filesystems I care about"
match = { fs_type = ["ext4", "exfat"] }

[[actions]]                    # one device
name = "run\-backup"
device = "backup\-disk"
on = ["mounted"]
run = ["~/bin/backup.sh", "{mountpoint}"]
timeout = 600

[[actions]]                    # several devices
device = ["yubikey", "backup\-disk"]
on = ["connected", "disconnected"]
run = ["notify\-send", "{device} {event}"]

[[actions]]                    # a tag, including unnamed things
tag = "storage"
on = ["mounted"]
allow_unnamed = true
run = ["notify\-send", "Drive mounted ({tag})"]

[[actions]]                    # warn about a pulled drive
device = "backup\-disk"
on = ["removed"]
run = ["notify\-send", "\-u", "critical", "Pulled without ejecting"]

[[actions]]                    # act on the kind of unmount
device = "backup\-disk"
on = ["unmounted"]
run = ["sh", "\-c",
       'if [ "$1" = 1 ]; then echo pulled; else echo ejected; fi',
       "sh", "{surprise}"]
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/udevmon/config.toml
The configuration.
.TP
.I ~/.config/udevmon/config.toml.bak
The previous version, written by every editing command.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR udevmon (1),
.BR "TOML" " (https://toml.io)"
